            "id": "m01",
            "title": "Foundation of Vijayanagara (1336 AD): Harihara, Bukka & Vidyaranya",
            "summary": "Decline of Sultanate power in the Deccan, Harihara I and Bukka I of the Sangama dynasty, spiritual guidance of Sage Vidyaranya, reconversion from Islam to Hinduism, and the consecration of Vijayanagara (Hampi) on the Tungabhadra.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n        <h4>Historical Background: The Collapse of Tughlaq Power in the South</h4>\n        <p>During the 1330s, the erratic tyranny and administrative failures of Sultan Muhammad bin Tughlaq triggered massive rebellions across peninsular India. In 1335 AD, the governor of Mabar (Tamil Nadu), <strong>Jalaluddin Ahsan Shah</strong>, broke away to found the independent <strong>Sultanate of Madurai</strong>. Simultaneously, the kingdoms of the Krishna-Tungabhadra basin rose in armed defiance against northern rule.</p>\n\n        <h4>Harihara and Bukka: From Captives to Founders</h4>\n        <p>The founders of the Vijayanagara Empire were two brothers, <strong>Harihara I (Hakka)</strong> and <strong>Bukka I</strong>, sons of Sangama:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Early Allegiance:</strong> The brothers served initially as treasury officers and feudatories under the <strong>Kakatiyas of Warangal</strong> (Prataparudra II). Following the fall of Warangal in 1323 AD, they entered the service of the kingdom of <strong>Kampili</strong> (near modern Bellary, Karnataka).</li>\n            <li><strong>Captivity and Forced Conversion:</strong> When Muhammad bin Tughlaq invaded and annexed Kampili in 1327 AD (for sheltering a rebel nephew, Bahauddin Gurshasp), Harihara and Bukka were captured, taken to Delhi in chains, and forcibly converted to Islam.</li>\n            <li>In 1334 AD, as rebellions engulfed Karnataka, the Sultan dispatched the brothers back to the south as imperial governors to restore Sultanate authority.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>The Spiritual Guidance of Sage Vidyaranya & Reconversion (1336 AD)</h4>\n        <p>Upon returning to the banks of the Tungabhadra River, the brothers met the celebrated Advaita scholar and sage, <strong>Swami Vidyaranya</strong> (and his brother Sayana, the great commentator on the Vedas) of the <strong>Sringeri Sharada Peetham</strong>:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Vidyaranya persuaded the brothers to renounce their allegiance to the Delhi Sultanate, undergo ritual purification (<em>Shuddhi</em>), and return to the Hindu faith.</li>\n            <li>He inspired them to establish an independent sovereign Hindu empire dedicated to protecting Sanatana Dharma, preserving Vedic culture, and defending southern India against foreign devastation.</li>\n            <li><strong>Foundation of the Capital (1336 AD):</strong> In <strong>1336 AD</strong>, on the southern bank of the <strong>Tungabhadra River</strong> opposite the ancient citadel of Anegundi, Harihara and Bukka laid the foundations of <strong>Vidyanagara</strong> (named in honor of Sage Vidyaranya), which rapidly grew into the world-famous imperial metropolis of <strong>Vijayanagara</strong> ('The City of Victory'), centered around the sacred shrine of <strong>Hampi (Pampa Kshetra)</strong>.</li>\n            <li><strong>Reign as Vassals of God Virupaksha:</strong> The Sangama brothers displayed profound spiritual humility: they ruled not as independent autocrats, but as direct earthly regents and deputies (<em>Karyakarta</em>) of the patron deity, <strong>Lord Virupaksha</strong> (a form of Shiva). All official royal orders, decrees, and land grants were signed in Kannada script as <strong>'Sri Virupaksha'</strong>, rather than in the King's personal name.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>Rapid Consolidation</h4>\n        <p>By 1346 AD, Harihara I peacefully absorbed the dying Hoysala kingdom following the death of Vira Ballala III and his son. Bukka I's son, Prince <strong>Kampana</strong>, led a triumphant expedition into Tamil Nadu, defeating the Sultan of Madurai in 1371 AD and restoring worship at the ancient Ranganathaswamy temple at Srirangam, an epic achievement immortalized by Kampana's wife, <strong>Gangadevi</strong>, in her Sanskrit epic <strong>Madhura Vijayam</strong>.</p>\n        ",
