            "title": "The Four Dynasties of Vijayanagara: Chronology & Transitions",
            "summary": "Chronological evolution of the four ruling dynasties: Sangama (1336–1485), Saluva (1485–1505), Tuluva (1505–1570), and Aravidu (1570–1646); the First Usurpation by Saluva Narasimha, and the Second Usurpation by Vira Narasimha.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n        <h4>The Dynastic Framework of Vijayanagara (1336–1646 AD)</h4>\n        <p>The three-century history of the Vijayanagara Empire spans four distinct ruling dynasties, each responding to shifting geopolitical challenges, military coups, and internal crises:</p>\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Sangama Dynasty (1336–1485 AD):</strong>\n                <ul>\n                    <li>Founded by Harihara I and Bukka I. Consolidation of Karnataka, expansion into Tamil Nadu, and early wars with the Bahmanis.</li>\n                    <li>Prominent rulers: Harihara II (assumed imperial title <em>Maharajadhiraja</em>), Deva Raya I, and <strong>Deva Raya II (Proudha Deva Raya)</strong>.</li>\n                    <li>Decline: Following the death of Mallikarjuna and the incompetent rule of <strong>Virupaksha Raya II</strong>, the empire faced military collapse against the Gajapatis and Bahmanis.</li>\n                </ul>\n            </li>\n            <li><strong>Saluva Dynasty (1485–1505 AD): The First Usurpation</strong>\n                <ul>\n                    <li><strong>Saluva Narasimha Deva Raya:</strong> A powerful military commander and governor of Chandragiri. Realizing that the degenerate Sangama dynasty could not defend the state, Narasimha marched to Hampi in 1485 AD, expelled Virupaksha II, and seized the throne to save the empire from dissolution—an event known as the <strong>First Usurpation</strong>.</li>\n                    <li>Saluva Narasimha restored military discipline, encouraged horse imports, and checked the Gajapatis. He died in 1491 AD, leaving his young sons under the regency of general <strong>Narasa Nayaka</strong>.</li>\n                </ul>\n            </li>\n            <li><strong>Tuluva Dynasty (1505–1570 AD): The Second Usurpation & Golden Age</strong>\n                <ul>\n                    <li><strong>Vira Narasimha:</strong> Son of Narasa Nayaka, deposed the puppet Saluva ruler Immadi Narasimha in 1505 AD, establishing the Tuluva Dynasty (the <strong>Second Usurpation</strong>).</li>\n                    <li><strong>Emperor Krishnadevaraya (1509–1529 AD):</strong> Half-brother of Vira Narasimha; under his reign the empire reached its glorious zenith of military power, territorial expansion, economic prosperity, and literary renaissance.</li>\n                    <li>Succeeded by Achyuta Deva Raya (1529–1542 AD) and Sadashiva Raya (1542–1570 AD), during whose nominal reign de facto power was seized by the regent <strong>Aliya Rama Raya</strong>.</li>\n                    <li>Disaster of Talikota (1565 AD) brought the catastrophic sacking of Hampi and the collapse of Tuluva power.</li>\n                </ul>\n            </li>\n            <li><strong>Aravidu Dynasty (1570–1646 AD): The Final Twilight</strong>\n                <ul>\n                    <li>Founded by <strong>Tirumala Raya</strong> (brother of Rama Raya), who abandoned the ruins of Hampi and relocated the capital south to <strong>Penukonda</strong> (Andhra Pradesh).</li>\n                    <li>Capital later shifted to <strong>Chandragiri</strong> and Vellore under <strong>Venkata II (1586–1614 AD)</strong>, who revitalized the empire.</li>\n                    <li>Finally dissolved in 1646 AD under Sriranga III due to internal rebellions by the Nayakas of Madurai, Thanjavur, and Gingee, and invasions by Bijapur and Golconda.</li>\n                </ul>\n            </li>\n        </ol>\n        ",
